<h2>Contents</h2>
<ol><li>Executive summary</li><li>Problem, context and relevance</li><li>Scope, users and requirements</li><li>Solution architecture and engineering decisions</li><li>Data model and API design</li><li>Four-agent workflow and safety case</li><li>Cross-platform use case</li><li>Third-party integration</li><li>Security, privacy and ethical boundaries</li><li>Testing, AI evaluation and performance evidence</li><li>Deployment and operational readiness</li><li>Assignment and marking-criteria traceability</li><li>Limitations and prioritized completion plan</li><li>References</li><li>Group AI declaration</li><li>Individual report sections (four students)</li></ol>

<h1>1. Executive summary</h1>
<p>LifeLink is a full-stack prototype for coordinating blood-request triage, blood-bank inventory, donor availability, donation camps and human-reviewed dispatch proposals. It brings four user-facing roles—donor, hospital requester, blood-bank administrator and camp operations—onto a shared system. The central assessed scenario begins when a hospital submits an urgent request in Flutter. The ASP.NET Core API validates and persists it in PostgreSQL, calls an internal multi-agent service to analyse stock and eligible donor candidates, stores the workflow trace and approval state, then presents the proposal in the React administrative console. An authorized administrator approves, rejects or requests revision. Only after approval does the backend carry out permitted business side effects and make the updated state available to the initiating hospital user.</p>
<p>The project is intentionally designed so that AI proposes and organizes work while deterministic software and authorized people retain operational authority. Four agents have separate contracts: coordinator/planner, domain analyst, dispatch proposer and validation/safety. Tools are allow-listed, proposal inputs and outputs are structured, and the final safety gate is deterministic. The agent service cannot directly reserve stock, notify donors or write application data; the API owns these effects.</p>
<p>The domain is relevant because safe blood services require coordinated donor management, sufficient availability, traceability and quality systems. The World Health Organization reports persistent global inequalities in access and recommends coordinated national blood systems and effective donor management [1]. At the same time, Sri Lanka’s National Blood Transfusion Service already documents a nationwide Blood Bank Management System, daily stock monitoring, a monthly statistics system and the Raktha donor appointment application [2]. LifeLink therefore must be understood as an academic prototype exploring additional cross-organization request-to-response coordination, not as a replacement for NBTS or evidence that Sri Lanka lacks blood-service technology.</p>
<p>Repository evidence shows substantial implementation breadth and written architecture decisions. However, the project’s own completion plan records important incomplete items: real PostgreSQL concurrency tests, durable agent recovery/idempotency, mobile and web feature gaps, security scanning, physical-device verification, live deployment evidence, and performance testing. This report distinguishes implemented artifacts from unverified outcomes and does not claim clinical validation, standards certification or production readiness.</p>
<div class="callout"><b>Project proposition:</b> reduce coordination friction and improve auditability in an urgent request workflow by sharing validated request, stock and donor-availability information, while preserving human authorization and existing clinical governance.</div>

<h1>2. Problem, context and relevance</h1>
<h2>2.1 The coordination problem</h2>
<p>Blood availability is a time-sensitive coordination problem involving a requesting care facility, blood-bank inventory, donor recruitment, eligibility and communication. A request is useful only when it can be translated into an actionable view of compatible, unexpired stock; a shortage must be visible; candidate donor outreach must respect suitability and consent; and every proposed and approved action must be traceable. Fragmented information, inconsistent status updates or unrecorded handoffs can create avoidable delay, duplicated work, uncertainty about stock, and difficulty reconstructing decisions after an incident.</p>
<p>These statements describe the design problem LifeLink addresses; they are not a measured finding about current Sri Lankan hospital practices. The project’s original narrative references phone, WhatsApp and informal coordination, but no field study or interview evidence is included in the repository. Before making those claims as facts, the team should add stakeholder interviews or a documented workflow study and cite the evidence. In particular, NBTS already operates digital systems that cover important parts of the blood lifecycle [2]. LifeLink’s credible research question is therefore: <i>can a governed cross-platform request and approval workflow add useful coordination, visibility or interoperability around existing systems?</i></p>
<h2>2.2 Why the problem matters</h2>
<p>WHO guidance emphasizes timely access to safe and sufficient blood products, coordinated national systems, regular voluntary unpaid donation, donor management, quality-assured screening, compatibility testing, and quality systems [1]. The operational relevance for software engineering is direct: data must be current; stock lots and expiry matter; allocation needs deterministic rules; donor information is sensitive; and failures need explicit escalation. LifeLink focuses on coordination support for request triage, candidate analysis, stock proposals, approval and audit. It does not screen blood, establish clinical compatibility for a patient, assess a person medically, recommend transfusion, or replace clinician or blood-bank decisions.</p>
<h2>2.3 Stakeholders and value hypothesis</h2>
<table><tr><th>Stakeholder</th><th>Need / friction addressed</th><th>Potential value to validate</th></tr>
<tr><td>Hospital requester</td><td>Submit a structured request, identify urgency and required-by time, follow its status and see a review outcome.</td><td>Less uncertainty during hand-offs; clearer status visibility.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blood-bank administrator</td><td>Review inventory, donors, camps and a traceable proposal in one operational console.</td><td>Faster triage and better documented decision-making.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Donor</td><td>Maintain profile/availability, interact with camp and request flows, receive notifications where enabled.</td><td>More organized, consent-respecting engagement and less irrelevant contact.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Camp coordinator / operations staff</td><td>Schedule camps and slots, manage roster and attendance.</td><td>More visible capacity and attendance records.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Existing national blood-service operators</td><td>Preserve authoritative processes, data ownership, safety rules and existing systems.</td><td>Interoperability only if formally approved and technically feasible.</td></tr></table>
<p>These are value hypotheses, not proven outcomes. A pilot would need user research and operational measures such as time from request submission to first review, time to a human decision, stale-stock rate, donor notification relevance, failed notification rate and workflow completion rate. Baselines and denominators must be captured before reporting improvement.</p>
<h2>2.4 Intended boundary</h2>
<p>LifeLink is an academic coordination prototype. Any real deployment would require authorization from relevant Sri Lankan health authorities and participating institutions, transfusion-medicine review, privacy and security assessment, integration approval, and a controlled pilot. Synthetic data is the only appropriate data for development and demonstration under the project documentation.</p>

<h1>3. Scope, users and requirements</h1>
<h2>3.1 Roles and authority</h2>
<table><tr><th>Role</th><th>Primary application</th><th>Responsibilities and constraints</th></tr>
<tr><td>Donor</td><td>Flutter mobile</td><td>Own profile, eligibility-related information and camp participation. The prototype does not provide clinical eligibility certification.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hospital requester</td><td>Flutter mobile</td><td>Submit and track requests for an associated hospital; must be authenticated, and hospital verification is an administrative control.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blood-bank administrator</td><td>React web</td><td>Manage inventory, donors, camps and hospital verification; inspect agent execution; approve, reject or revise high-impact proposals.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Camp operations</td><td>React / mobile workflows as implemented</td><td>Manage camp lifecycle, slots, roster and attendance according to role-authorized operations.</td></tr></table>
<h2>3.2 Four business components and ownership model</h2>
<p>The architecture decision record maps the four student ownership areas to distinct domain components and agents. Student numbers are placeholders for legal names; repository materials explicitly say the mapping must be completed by the group. The assignment requires each student to own a component and make a technical and agentic contribution across the stack.</p>
