<tr><td>Integrity</td><td>PostgreSQL relational model, EF Core migrations, constraints/indexes, lot-based reservations, history records, idempotency fields.</td><td>Model exists; real-PostgreSQL concurrency and rollback evidence remains outstanding per completion plan.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Auditability</td><td>Workflow execution, ordered steps, tool payloads, approval records, correlation IDs and domain histories.</td><td>Persisted structures exist; production-grade immutable audit/retention policy and recovery validation remain incomplete.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Availability / graceful failure</td><td>Health checks, bounded model calls, deterministic fallback, safe outcomes, notification outbox worker.</td><td>Unit-level resilience covered; service outages, durable restart recovery, delivery retries and operational SLOs need evidence.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Usability / accessibility</td><td>Distinct admin web and field mobile use, responsive layouts, loading/error states, focus and reduced-motion styling.</td><td>Design work documented; formal WCAG 2.2 assessment not recorded.</td></tr></table>

<h1>4. Solution architecture and engineering decisions</h1>
<h2>4.1 Component architecture</h2>
<table><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Technology</th><th>Responsibility and boundary</th></tr>
<tr><td>Field client</td><td>Flutter, Dart, Riverpod</td><td>Donor and hospital tasks, device location/notifications, typed API integration. It does not own business rules or call agents directly.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Administrative client</td><td>React, TypeScript, Vite, React Router, TanStack Query</td><td>Admin operations, data management, workflow review and decision. It calls the same public API as Flutter.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Public application boundary</td><td>ASP.NET Core 8 Web API</td><td>Authentication, authorization, validation, deterministic domain services, persistence and external-service boundary.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Domain/application/infrastructure</td><td>C# layered solution, EF Core</td><td>Domain entities/invariants; application contracts; infrastructure services, workers and data mapping.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Relational persistence</td><td>PostgreSQL 16</td><td>Identity, hospitals, donors, requests, inventory, reservations, camps, notifications and workflow audit state.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Internal proposal service</td><td>Python, FastAPI, LangGraph; Ollama optional</td><td>Bounded plan and analysis graph, allow-listed pure proposal tools and structured result. Not an independently exposed client API.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Push provider</td><td>Firebase Cloud Messaging (optional configuration)</td><td>Backend-side push delivery integration; privileged service account belongs only in backend environment.</td></tr></table>
<h3>System architecture diagram</h3>
<table class="diagram"><tr><td class="box">Flutter mobile<br/><span style="font-weight:normal">Donor + Hospital</span></td><td class="arrow">HTTPS / JSON →</td><td class="layer" rowspan="2"><b>ASP.NET Core 8 API</b><br/>JWT + RBAC · DTO validation<br/>Domain/application services<br/>Workflow orchestration · audit<br/>Post-approval side effects</td><td class="arrow">EF Core ↔</td><td class="store"><b>PostgreSQL</b><br/>Users · requests · inventory<br/>Reservations · camps<br/>Agent state · approvals</td></tr>
<tr><td class="box">React web<br/><span style="font-weight:normal">Blood-bank admin</span></td><td class="arrow">HTTPS / JSON →</td><td class="arrow">Internal authenticated call ↔</td><td class="agent"><b>Python / FastAPI</b><br/><b>LangGraph proposal service</b><br/>Coordinator → Analysis →<br/>Dispatch → Safety validation<br/><i>No direct database or side-effect access</i></td></tr>
<tr><td class="box">Flutter status refresh</td><td class="arrow">← shared API</td><td class="layer"><b>Same API authority</b><br/>Reads updated request state<br/>after the reviewed decision</td><td class="arrow">Approved effects →</td><td class="store">Reservation / dispatch records<br/>Notification outbox</td></tr>
<tr><td class="box">FCM device SDK</td><td class="arrow">← push delivery</td><td class="layer">Backend notification provider<br/>and bounded outbox worker</td><td class="arrow">HTTPS →</td><td class="agent">Firebase Cloud Messaging<br/><i>Optional third-party service</i></td></tr></table>
<p class="small">Trust boundary: React and Flutter call only the ASP.NET Core API. The API calls the internal proposal service using a service credential and owns all business writes. Agent results return through the API and must pass contract and deterministic safety validation. Firebase credentials remain server-side.</p>
<p><b>Trust boundary:</b> both clients → ASP.NET Core API → PostgreSQL and internal agent. Only the API performs application writes and post-approval side effects. The agent receives a bounded request payload and returns a validated structured proposal. The database is not called by React, Flutter or the Python agent directly.</p>
<h2>4.2 Architecture choices and trade-offs</h2>
<ul><li><b>ASP.NET Core as system authority:</b> required by the assignment and valuable for consistent validation, access control and audit boundaries across web and mobile.</li><li><b>PostgreSQL + EF Core:</b> relational integrity suits linked requests, hospitals, users, inventory lots, reservations and histories; migrations make schema evolution reviewable. JSONB is limited to versioned agent payloads, not used to claim every field is fully normalized.</li><li><b>React Context + TanStack Query:</b> small session context and server-state cache with invalidation/polling for operational dashboards; API remains authoritative.</li><li><b>Flutter Riverpod:</b> explicit, testable application state; typed service boundary; secure storage for refresh tokens, with short-lived access credentials held in memory according to ADR-03.</li><li><b>LangGraph fixed graph:</b> explicit four-agent orchestration is easier to inspect and bound than an open-ended autonomous loop. Model output cannot choose arbitrary code or tools.</li><li><b>Deterministic fallback:</b> keeps local and CI operation independent of a hosted model and ensures the model is not a prerequisite for domain correctness.</li><li><b>Deployment:</b> ADR-06 selects Vercel, Render and Neon, with Docker Compose for local/integration use; deployment configuration and evaluator URLs are not present as verified deliverables in the reviewed completion plan.</li></ul>
<h2>4.3 Architecture flow</h2>
<table><tr><th>Sequence</th><th>Action</th><th>Component and durable result</th></tr>
<tr><td>1. Request</td><td>Hospital user submits blood type, quantity, urgency and required-by time.</td><td>Flutter sends authenticated REST request to API.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2. Validate and persist</td><td>API checks identity, hospital authorization, input constraints and request state.</td><td>Request and status history saved in PostgreSQL.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3. Start workflow</td><td>API creates correlated workflow execution and calls internal service with bounded data.</td><td>Agent service key is service-to-service only; client has no agent credential.</td></tr>
<tr><td>4. Propose</td><td>Coordinator plans; analysis tool evaluates candidates/lots; dispatch tool proposes allocation and outreach.</td><td>Structured plan, ordered steps and tool inputs/results returned.</td></tr>
<tr><td>5. Validate</td><td>Deterministic schema/business/safety checks decide pending approval, escalation or safe failure.</td><td>API validates response contract and persists trace/state.</td></tr>
<tr><td>6. Review</td><td>Administrator inspects workflow in React and approves, rejects or requests revision.</td><td>Role-protected decision and reviewer are stored.</td></tr>
<tr><td>7. Execute after approval</td><td>Backend revalidates current stock and request state, then applies reservations and notifications.</td><td>Domain records/outbox updated; conflict or unavailable stock yields explicit failure/review.</td></tr>
<tr><td>8. Close the loop</td><td>Hospital user refreshes status and sees the outcome.</td><td>Same API and PostgreSQL data serves both platforms.</td></tr></table>

<h1>5. Data model and API design</h1>
<h2>5.1 Relational model</h2>
<p>The reviewed ER model contains User, Donor, Hospital, HospitalStaff, RefreshToken, DeviceToken, DonationRecord, DonorEligibilityHistory, BloodRequest, RequestStatusHistory, BloodBankLocation, InventoryLot, InventoryReservation, DispatchRecord, DonationCamp, CampSlot, AgentWorkflowExecution, AgentStep, AgentApproval and Notification. The key relationships preserve ownership and history: a hospital submits requests; a request may have multiple numbered workflow attempts; a workflow contains ordered steps and approval decisions; inventory lots are reserved through explicit reservation records; camps offer slots that donors may book.</p>
<h3>Editable EER diagram</h3>
<p>PK denotes primary key and FK denotes foreign key. The grouped entity boxes below show the implemented relational concepts; the relationship register underneath gives the cardinality and FK path for each connector. This layout is editable in Word and corresponds to the reviewed model in <i>docs/er-diagram.md</i>.</p>
<table class="diagram"><tr><th colspan="3">IDENTITY AND ORGANIZATIONS</th></tr>
<tr><td class="box"><b>User</b><br/>PK Id<br/>Email (unique), PasswordHash, Role</td><td class="box"><b>Hospital</b><br/>PK Id<br/>RegistrationNumber (unique), VerificationStatus</td><td class="box"><b>BloodBankLocation</b><br/>PK Id<br/>Name, Address, coordinates</td></tr>
<tr><td class="box"><b>Donor</b><br/>PK Id · FK UserId (unique)<br/>BloodType, DateOfBirth, eligibility,<br/>location, medical flags</td><td class="box"><b>HospitalStaff</b><br/>PK Id · FK UserId (unique)<br/>FK HospitalId<br/>Position</td><td class="box"><b>RefreshToken / DeviceToken</b><br/>PK Id · FK UserId<br/>Token hash / device token,<br/>expiry, revocation or active state</td></tr>
<tr><th colspan="3">REQUEST, INVENTORY AND AUDIT</th></tr>
<tr><td class="box"><b>BloodRequest</b><br/>PK Id · FK HospitalId<br/>FK RequestedByUserId<br/>BloodType, quantity, urgency, status</td><td class="box"><b>InventoryLot</b><br/>PK Id · FK LocationId<br/>BloodType, units, expiry, status, version</td><td class="box"><b>AgentWorkflowExecution</b><br/>PK Id · FK BloodRequestId<br/>AttemptNumber, PlanJson, status,<br/>CorrelationId, outcome</td></tr>
<tr><td class="box"><b>RequestStatusHistory</b><br/>PK Id · FK BloodRequestId<br/>FK ChangedByUserId<br/>Previous/New status, reason, time</td><td class="box"><b>InventoryReservation</b><br/>PK Id · FK BloodRequestId<br/>FK InventoryLotId<br/>Units, expiry, unique idempotency key</td><td class="box"><b>AgentStep / AgentApproval</b><br/>PK Id · FK WorkflowExecutionId<br/>Sequence / approval version,<br/>structured payload / decision</td></tr>
<tr><th colspan="3">DONOR ENGAGEMENT AND CAMPS</th></tr>
<tr><td class="box"><b>DonationRecord</b><br/>PK Id · FK DonorId<br/>DonationDate, Units, Location</td><td class="box"><b>DonationCamp</b><br/>PK Id<br/>Status, capacity, dates, location</td><td class="box"><b>DispatchRecord / Notification</b><br/>PK Id · FK ReservationId / UserId<br/>ApprovedByUserId, delivery state,<br/>idempotency key</td></tr>
<tr><td class="box"><b>DonorEligibilityHistory</b><br/>PK Id · FK DonorId<br/>Previous/New status, reason,<br/>ChangedByUserId</td><td class="box"><b>CampSlot</b><br/>PK Id · FK DonationCampId<br/>FK DonorId (nullable until booked)<br/>SlotTime, booking/attendance status</td><td class="box"><b>Relationships (cardinality)</b><br/>1 User → 0..1 Donor / HospitalStaff<br/>1 Hospital → many staff / requests<br/>1 Workflow → many Steps / Approvals<br/>1 Lot → many Reservations</td></tr></table>
<h3>EER relationship register</h3>
<table><tr><th>Parent / child</th><th>Cardinality</th><th>Foreign key / integrity meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>User → Donor</td><td>1 → 0..1</td><td>Donor.UserId is unique; a donor profile belongs to one account.</td></tr>
<tr><td>User → HospitalStaff</td><td>1 → 0..1</td><td>HospitalStaff.UserId is unique; staff access is associated with a hospital.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hospital → HospitalStaff / BloodRequest</td><td>1 → many</td><td>HospitalStaff.HospitalId and BloodRequest.HospitalId.</td></tr>
<tr><td>User → RefreshToken / DeviceToken / Notification</td><td>1 → many</td><td>Credential/device/recipient rows belong to an account; secrets are stored/handled according to their dedicated policy.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Donor → eligibility history / donations / camp bookings</td><td>1 → many</td><td>Each historical record or booked slot identifies its donor.</td></tr>
<tr><td>BloodRequest → status history / workflow executions / reservations</td><td>1 → many</td><td>Attempt number is unique per request; histories preserve lifecycle and allocation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>BloodBankLocation → InventoryLot → InventoryReservation</td><td>1 → many at each stage</td><td>Lot-based stock with independently tracked reserved units, status and expiry.</td></tr>
<tr><td>InventoryReservation → DispatchRecord</td><td>1 → 0..1</td><td>A reservation can become an auditable dispatch record after approval.</td></tr>
<tr><td>AgentWorkflowExecution → AgentStep / AgentApproval</td><td>1 → many</td><td>Ordered execution trace and versioned human decisions are associated with an attempt.</td></tr>
<tr><td>DonationCamp → CampSlot</td><td>1 → many</td><td>Slots belong to a camp; capacity and booking state require transactional enforcement.</td></tr></table>
<p class="small">The diagram is a report-level view, not a substitute for the full EF Core model/migrations. Confirm precise nullability, indexes, delete behavior and constraints against the current LifeLinkDbContext before submission.</p>
<p>Invariants recorded by the project include unique email, hospital registration number, device token, reservation and notification idempotency keys, and correlation identifiers; at most one donor or hospital-staff profile per user; unique request/attempt number; lot-based expiring reservations; restricted deletes for audit-linked records. These are important design intentions; their database migration and race behavior should be demonstrated against real PostgreSQL.</p>
<h2>5.2 Data minimization and lifecycle</h2>
<p>The domain can involve address and approximate coordinates, date of birth, blood type, medical flags, donation history, hospital details, push tokens and request notes. These fields require purpose limitation and role-based minimization. Store only what is needed for the prototype; use synthetic data; keep medical free-text out of agent prompts where possible; do not put sensitive clinical details in push notification bodies, ordinary logs, URLs or model traces. Set documented retention, correction and deletion rules before any real-data pilot. The workflow stores structured inputs and outputs needed for review; raw chain-of-thought, passwords, access tokens and provider secrets are prohibited by design documentation.</p>
<h2>5.3 API conventions</h2>
<p>REST controllers expose authentication, donors, hospitals, requests, inventory, camps, notifications and agent workflows. The API uses request/response DTOs, asynchronous operations, server-side validation, ProblemDetails error envelopes, role authorization policies and ownership checks. Workflow endpoints include start, list/get, execution summary, approve, reject and revise. Expected statuses include accepted for asynchronous workflow start, success responses for reads and decisions, 401/403 for authentication/authorization, 404 for missing resources, 409 for lifecycle/concurrency conflicts and 422 for invalid workflow output (as mapped in the controller).</p>
<p>Before final submission, publish the generated OpenAPI document and map each component’s four meaningful endpoints and domain-specific operation to the marking checklist. The project completion plan identifies API versioning/frozen OpenAPI as a remaining quality task.</p>
<h2>5.4 Illustrative schema invariants</h2>
<table><tr><th>Invariant</th><th>Why it matters</th><th>Verification to retain</th></tr>
<tr><td>Unique (request_id, attempt_number)</td><td>Prevents ambiguous retry history.</td><td>Migration/constraint test and concurrent start test.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reservation idempotency key unique</td><td>Prevents duplicate stock reservation on retried approval.</td><td>Repeat request and worker restart tests.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Available lot units cannot become negative</td><td>Protects stock integrity under concurrent proposals.</td><td>Database constraint plus simultaneous reservation integration test.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Approval references an authorized admin</td><td>Preserves accountable human decision authority.</td><td>RBAC/ownership API tests and audit trace.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Camp capacity is not exceeded</td><td>Avoids overbooking and operational conflict.</td><td>Concurrent booking integration test in PostgreSQL.</td></tr></table>

<h1>6. Four-agent workflow and safety case</h1>
<h2>6.1 Why agents are used</h2>
<p>The selected task has sequential responsibilities: interpret an objective, analyze domain evidence, produce operational proposals and validate them. Distinct agents make those responsibilities visible and separately testable. The multi-agent design is not claimed to improve clinical accuracy by itself. Its purpose is modular orchestration, transparent hand-offs and clear permission boundaries. Deterministic algorithms handle blood-group compatibility, expiry/allocation, tool input checking and safety rules; any language model is bounded to structured planning and can fall back to deterministic planning.</p>
<h2>6.2 Agent contracts, permissions and outputs</h2>
<table><tr><th>Agent</th><th>Input</th><th>Responsibility/output</th><th>Tools/permission boundary</th></tr>
<tr><td>Coordinator / Planning</td><td>Request identifier, blood type, quantity and urgency.</td><td>Creates a structured, bounded plan and sends work to the designated stages; records selected model/fallback.</td><td>No database, notification or dispatch authority. Planner steps must be allow-listed and schema-valid.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Domain Analysis</td><td>Request facts, candidate donor summary, inventory-lot summary.</td><td>Counts/identifies compatible donor candidates and available compatible lots; treats request notes as untrusted text.</td><td>Pure candidate-analysis tool; no network or persistence access.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dispatch</td><td>Validated compatibility and shortage results.</td><td>Produces FEFO-style lot allocation proposal and a donor-outreach proposal only where shortage remains.</td><td>Proposal-only tools; cannot reserve lots, contact donors or execute arbitrary HTTP/code.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Validation and Safety</td><td>Final structured proposal and request constraints.</td><td>Deterministically evaluates safety/approval conditions and returns PendingApproval, EscalationRequired or a safe outcome.</td><td>Deterministic gate; cannot be overridden by a model’s claim that approval is unnecessary.</td></tr></table>
<h2>6.3 Workflow state and audit</h2>
<p>The API persists a workflow identifier, request identifier, attempt number, objective, plan JSON, correlation ID, status, ordered agent steps, structured input/output/tool-call data, timestamps, error fields, final outcome and versioned approval records. This gives administrators an execution summary and supports request-to-decision reconstruction. Structured payloads are JSON validated against versioned boundary schemas. The system does not persist raw chain-of-thought. Durable LangGraph checkpoint/resume and idempotent graph retries are explicitly recorded as unfinished; persisted API summaries should not be confused with full crash-resumable agent checkpoints.</p>
<h2>6.4 Human approval is an actual control boundary</h2>
<p>High-impact proposals pause in a pending-approval state. Only an authorized blood-bank administrator can approve, reject or request revision through the backend. On approval, the API must re-check request status and stock because facts may have changed while waiting. Only backend business services perform reservations, dispatch records or donor notifications after the decision. In a shortage or unsafe/unsupported case, the workflow should escalate or fail safely rather than improvise. For a real system, approval must remain a meaningful review by trained staff and must not be presented as clinical authorization unless the governance body defines it as such.</p>
<h2>6.5 Threats and controls</h2>
<table><tr><th>Threat</th><th>Control represented in design</th><th>Residual work</th></tr>
<tr><td>Prompt injection in request notes</td><td>Notes treated as untrusted data; no free-form tool authority; golden prompt-attack scenarios.</td><td>Expand direct/indirect adversarial tests and prove no tool/approval bypass across model versions.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hallucinated or malformed plan</td><td>Schema validation, allow-list and bounded deterministic fallback.</td><td>Record production metrics, malformed-output handling and versioned model evaluations.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Excessive agency / unauthorized side effects</td><td>Agent tools only propose; API enforces authorization and human approval for high-impact effects.</td><td>Test all side effects after approval, duplicate decision races and compromised-agent payloads.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stale stock or duplicate execution</td><td>Backend revalidation, reservations/idempotency fields, expiry worker.</td><td>Real PostgreSQL race, crash/retry and transaction-boundary evidence required.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Privacy leakage to model/logs/notifications</td><td>Bounded input and no raw reasoning persistence; credentials excluded from app payload.</td><td>Formal data-flow review, redaction tests, retention policy, provider/hosting review.</td></tr></table>
<h2>6.6 Agent evaluation design</h2>
<p>The repository describes a twelve-case golden corpus covering exact/partial/missing inventory, compatibility, expired/empty lots, donor ordering, rare/critical approval and prompt-injection attempts. Agent tests also cover malformed output, timeout, retry and rejection of non-allow-listed steps. Evaluation should use deterministic assertions rather than relying only on an LLM judge: plan schema, expected agent participation and order, tool-name allow-list, business-rule outputs, approval requirement, no unauthorized side effects, persisted evidence, fallback and safe-failure status. A final report should attach actual command output/CI run IDs, scenario-level results, model/provider/version, environment and failure analysis. The current report does not rerun or certify these tests.</p>

<h1>7. Cross-platform assessed use case</h1>
<h2>Urgent hospital request to approved response</h2>
<ol><li><b>Hospital requester in Flutter:</b> signs in and submits a typed request with urgency, quantity and required-by time. The app reports the created request ID and initial state.</li><li><b>API validates and persists:</b> validates role, hospital association, fields and lifecycle; stores request/status history in PostgreSQL.</li><li><b>Coordinator plans:</b> the internal agent receives a minimized structured objective and returns a bounded plan.</li><li><b>Analysis agent evaluates:</b> a deterministic tool examines supplied candidate and inventory summaries, including compatibility and expiry constraints.</li><li><b>Dispatch agent proposes:</b> tool proposes lots to reserve and, if a shortage remains, candidate recipients for outreach. It cannot execute the proposal.</li><li><b>Safety agent validates:</b> deterministic rules decide whether to request approval, escalate, or stop safely. Versioned contract validation occurs at the service boundary.</li><li><b>API persists the trace:</b> workflow, plan, ordered steps, tool calls, validation, correlation ID and outcome are written for review.</li><li><b>Administrator in React:</b> opens the workflow timeline, examines the proposal and approves, rejects or requests revision with comments.</li><li><b>Backend revalidates and executes:</b> current request/stock are checked; approved operations are applied via backend services and notification outbox/provider.</li><li><b>Flutter closes the loop:</b> hospital user sees the updated request state from the same API and shared PostgreSQL data.</li></ol>
<p><b>Failure paths:</b> if the agent times out, returns malformed data or requests a disallowed operation, the API records a safe failure/escalation and does not perform side effects. If stock changed after the proposal, the backend returns a conflict and requires a fresh review/revision. If notification delivery fails, the business decision and delivery attempt must be represented separately; operators need retry visibility.</p>
<div class="note"><b>Demonstration acceptance checklist:</b> show both actual clients; show the shared request ID and database state; show all four agents and tool outputs; show deterministic validation; show workflow persistence; show role-protected approval in React; show post-approval backend state; show the hospital’s updated status; demonstrate at least one safe failure. The assignment requires the application’s Agentic AI subsystem to run in the demo, with no external AI assistant used during the viva.</div>

<h1>8. Third-party integration</h1>
<p>Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM) is the implemented optional push-notification provider. It supports timely donor/camp/request communication on mobile and satisfies the assignment’s requirement for a meaningful external service when enabled and demonstrated. Provider credentials are configured on the API side as a base64 service-account JSON value; the mobile client registers a device token but must never contain service-account credentials. If FCM is disabled, an auditable logging provider supports local development, but it does not demonstrate real push delivery.</p>
<table><tr><th>Integration concern</th><th>Design / evidence to show</th></tr>
<tr><td>Business purpose</td><td>Deliver relevant updates to users where the application and user consent/settings permit.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Credential boundary</td><td>Secret remains in API runtime configuration/secret store; never in Git, Flutter bundle, browser bundle or report.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Failure handling</td><td>HTTP timeout, provider errors and outbox behavior; retain sanitized attempts and bounded retries.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data minimization</td><td>Push payload should avoid patient-identifiable and unnecessary medical details; direct the user to authenticated app views.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Show Firebase configuration state, sanitized delivery result and on-device notification flow; otherwise label integration as implemented/configurable, not live-verified.</td></tr></table>

<h1>9. Security, privacy and ethical boundaries</h1>
<h2>9.1 Application security</h2>
<p>Repository implementation includes JWT issuer/audience/signature/lifetime validation, HMAC-SHA256 algorithm restriction, role policies, password hashing, refresh-token rotation and revocation, rate limiting on credential endpoints, startup rejection of weak/placeholder secrets outside Development/Testing, CORS configuration, exception handling, ProblemDetails, correlation middleware and security response headers. These controls align with common secure API practices, but code presence alone is not proof of ASVS conformance or absence of vulnerabilities. The OWASP API Security Top 10 is a suitable risk checklist for object/function authorization, resource consumption, sensitive business flows, misconfiguration, API inventory and unsafe upstream consumption [3].</p>
<h2>9.2 Privacy and data governance</h2>
<p>LifeLink processes data that can be sensitive: donor identity/contact, blood group, date of birth, approximate location, medical flags, donation history, hospital affiliation, request details and device tokens. Before use with real people, perform data mapping and a purpose/retention assessment; minimize fields and access; specify correction/deletion and communications preferences; review third-party processors and hosting locations; protect backups and transport/storage encryption; define breach response; and assess current Sri Lankan obligations with qualified institutional/legal guidance. The Data Protection Authority lists the 2022 Act, 2025 Amendment and later gazettes/guidance [4]. This report does not interpret legal duties or declare compliance.</p>
<h2>9.3 Clinical and ethical boundaries</h2>
<ul><li>Blood compatibility and donor eligibility rules must be reviewed and version-controlled by qualified local transfusion professionals before operational use.</li><li>Software must not infer medical suitability from free text or present a donor-match score as a clinical decision.</li><li>Donor outreach must be opt-in, proportionate, privacy-preserving and subject to opt-out/contact-frequency controls.</li><li>Administrators need a clear explanation of why candidates/lots were proposed and a way to reject or correct the plan.</li><li>Urgency and equity rules need stakeholder governance so that geography, device access or profile completeness do not silently disadvantage users.</li><li>Fallback and outages must make limitations visible; a software recommendation must never delay established emergency escalation channels.</li></ul>
<h2>9.4 Security evidence still required</h2>
<p>Complete a route-by-route role/ownership matrix, security headers/TLS/proxy review, secret/dependency/static/container scanning, model and notification data-flow review, threat-model review, backup/key handling review and independent penetration test before real data. Publish an ASVS 5.0 checklist with status and evidence; do not claim a certification. OWASP ASVS 5.0 is a current application-security verification standard [5]. For the agent layer, use the OWASP 2025 LLM risks—including prompt injection, sensitive information disclosure, improper output handling and excessive agency—as adversarial test categories [6].</p>

