<h1>7. Technology Stack &amp; Justification</h1>$technologyBody
<h2>7.1 Selection rationale and alternatives</h2><table><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Why it fits</th><th>Trade-off / alternative considered</th></tr><tr><td>ASP.NET Core Web API</td><td>Mandatory stack and single authority for API, rules, persistence and integrations.</td><td>More explicit DTO/service structure than a rapid monolith; accepted to enforce clear layers and cross-client consistency.</td></tr><tr><td>PostgreSQL + EF Core</td><td>Relational integrity, transactions, migration history and suitable JSONB for versioned workflow payloads.</td><td>JSONB payloads require schema validation; relational data remains primary.</td></tr><tr><td>React + TanStack Query</td><td>Administrative screens are dominated by API-owned data, caching and mutation invalidation.</td><td>Context + query cache is lighter than Redux for current state needs; avoid treating cached data as authority.</td></tr><tr><td>Flutter + Riverpod</td><td>One mobile codebase, typed providers and testable state; supports device APIs.</td><td>Device-specific build/release and plugin behavior add validation work.</td></tr><tr><td>Python + LangGraph</td><td>Explicit graph makes agent roles and order visible; supports structured orchestration and bounded tools.</td><td>Additional internal service boundary; deterministic planner fallback avoids mandatory hosted model dependence.</td></tr></table>

<h1>8. Overall System Architecture</h1>$architectureBody
<h2>8.1 Main trust boundaries</h2><ul><li>Public clients call only the ASP.NET Core API.</li><li>The API calls the internal agent service using a dedicated service credential.</li><li>The agent receives bounded request context and returns structured proposals; it has no database credentials or execution side effects.</li><li>Only API business services modify request, reservation, dispatch and notification state.</li><li>External FCM delivery is invoked from the API-side provider/outbox path.</li></ul>

<h1>9. ASP.NET Core Backend Architecture</h1>
<h2>9.1 Layering and request path</h2><p>The solution separates LifeLink.Api, LifeLink.Application, LifeLink.Domain and LifeLink.Infrastructure. Controllers bind and validate transport DTOs; Application contains contracts and service abstractions; Domain entities encode lifecycle state; Infrastructure implements EF Core persistence, business services, HTTP clients and hosted workers. Dependency injection wires these boundaries. This keeps public transport concerns out of domain entities and makes services testable.</p>
<table><tr><th>Concern</th><th>Implementation responsibility</th></tr><tr><td>Identity/security</td><td>JWT bearer validation, role policies, account services, hashed refresh tokens, rate limits on credential routes and startup secret checks.</td></tr><tr><td>Domain operations</td><td>Donor, hospital, request, inventory, camp and workflow services validate transitions and ownership.</td></tr><tr><td>Persistence</td><td>LifeLinkDbContext and EF Core/Npgsql mappings, migrations, unique/check constraints, audit timestamps and concurrency/version fields.</td></tr><tr><td>Resilience</td><td>Global exception handler, ProblemDetails, correlation middleware, bounded HttpClient to agent and background expiry/outbox workers.</td></tr><tr><td>API documentation/health</td><td>Swagger/OpenAPI in Development; health endpoint; CI migration script artifact.</td></tr></table>
<h2>9.2 API surface and conventions</h2>$apiBody
<table><tr><th>Controller/resource group</th><th>Representative responsibility</th><th>Typical authorization boundary</th></tr><tr><td>Auth</td><td>Register, login, refresh, revoke, current profile</td><td>Anonymous throttled auth endpoints; authenticated profile</td></tr><tr><td>Donors</td><td>Profile, eligibility, history and administrative donor operations</td><td>Own donor profile or admin policy</td></tr><tr><td>Hospitals / Requests</td><td>Hospital verification and request lifecycle</td><td>Hospital requester ownership or admin</td></tr><tr><td>Inventory / Camps</td><td>Stock lots, reservations, camps, slots and attendance</td><td>Admin and relevant participant ownership</td></tr><tr><td>Notifications</td><td>Device token registration and notification functions</td><td>Authenticated user/device ownership</td></tr><tr><td>AgentWorkflows</td><td>Start/list/get workflow; approve/reject/revise</td><td>BloodBankAdmin for workflow review operations</td></tr></table>
<p>The workflow controller maps not-found to 404, lifecycle/concurrency conflicts to 409, invalid agent output to 422, and failed approval effects to 502. The exact endpoint catalogue, parameters, status codes and auth scheme should be captured from a current Swagger/OpenAPI export in the final report evidence.</p>
