<h1>11. React Web Application</h1>
<h2>11.1 Purpose and structure</h2><p>The React application is the operational console for blood-bank administrators. It provides dashboard views, donor and hospital administration, request and inventory operations, camps, and an agent-workflow monitor. React Router and protected routes separate authenticated screens; an authentication context holds the small session boundary; TanStack Query manages server state, refetching, invalidation and mutation feedback. Local filter/selection state remains close to the screen that uses it.</p>
<h2>11.2 Operational experience</h2><p>Pages represented in the repository include Dashboard, Donors, Donor Portal, Hospitals, Requests, Inventory, Camps and Workflows. Workflow review shows status, attempt number, execution steps, structured outputs/tool calls, and approve/reject/revise actions when approval is pending. Shared UI components provide shell/layout, query states, icons and theme switching. Forms include validation and feedback. The completion plan still identifies work around donor histories, reservation visibility, attendance reporting, pagination controls and conflict confirmation; mark each feature based on current code and actual browser evidence.</p>
<h2>11.3 State and API integration</h2><p>The browser calls the ASP.NET Core API through a centralized typed client. The backend remains authoritative: UI permissions and cached data improve usability but do not replace server authorization or revalidation.</p>
$(New-ShotSlot '04' 'React administration interface' 'Insert a current screenshot of the dashboard and a separate workflow approval timeline. Use seeded synthetic data; caption the route and role.')

<h1>12. Flutter Mobile Application</h1>
<h2>12.1 Purpose and user journeys</h2><p>Flutter supports donor and hospital-facing field workflows through the shared API. The documented implementation includes authentication/session restoration, donor profile and camp flows, hospital request creation/status, location fallback, Firebase token/message handling and secure refresh-token storage. Riverpod owns app/session state, a typed HTTP service centralizes requests, and protected routes reflect the authenticated role.</p>
<h2>12.2 Device capability and limitations</h2><p>Location and push notifications are the meaningful device/service features. Permission denial, disabled location, background delivery, notification taps and token revocation must be demonstrated on a real Android device. The completion plan records unfinished hospital request history/detail and physical-device/release validation. Clearly distinguish emulator evidence from device evidence, and do not claim release readiness until the signed APK is installed and exercised.</p>
<h2>12.3 Secure session handling</h2><p>ADR-03 records flutter_secure_storage for refresh tokens and in-memory access tokens. Explain refresh rotation, logout/revocation, API error handling and what happens offline. Never embed Firebase service-account credentials or API secrets in the mobile application.</p>
$(New-ShotSlot '05' 'Flutter mobile application' 'Insert current donor/home and hospital request/status screenshots, then a device-permission or notification screenshot if physically verified.')

<h1>13. Agentic AI Architecture</h1>$agentsBody
<h2>13.6 Model boundary and reliability</h2><p>The optional Ollama model is used for bounded planning rather than business authority. The deterministic planner is the mandatory fallback for malformed, unavailable or timed-out planning. Model attempts/timeouts are bounded; no raw chain-of-thought is persisted. API boundary schemas and deterministic tool outputs reduce the impact of a model error but do not eliminate prompt injection or bias risk.</p>
$(New-ShotSlot '06' 'Agent graph and persisted execution trace' 'Insert a LangGraph/orchestration view or, preferably, a real workflow execution screen showing all four distinct steps and structured tool results.')

